five days ago the nfc championship game  ended with one of the worst non-calls in  a postseason game that we can ever  remember the saints had the ball  deep in l.a rams territory was third  down and tommy lee lewis was just wiped  out by nikhil roby-coleman should have  been first in goal for the saints at  about the five-yard line with a minute  49 to play and the rams having one  timeout and the saints in position to  run the clock down kick a field goal and  give the rams very very limited time on  the clock to try to match it or beat it  that has been one of the biggest  controversies of the week there have  been developments every day and through  it all the commissioner has been  silent ben watson one of the most  respected players in the national  football league a member of the new  orleans saints posted this statement  yesterday on twitter commissioner  goodell we all realized that football  isn't a perfect game played coached and  officiated by imperfect people what  occurred last sunday in new orleans  though was outside of that expected and  accepted norm your continued silence on  this matter is unbecoming of the  position you hold detrimental to the  integrity of the game and disrespectful  and dismissive to football fans  everywhere from the locker room to park  avenue accountability is what makes our  league great lead by example  we are waiting  ben watson saints tight end saying what  a lot of saints fans and other fans  media and observers are thinking where  is roger goodell tom kern who's going to  join us coming up here in about 10  minutes talk patriots he calls roger  goodell the highest paid pin cushion in  the world because goodell is there to  take the flack that otherwise would go  to the owners in this case though it's a  siege mentality again at 345 park avenue  the kind of thing we first saw with the  ray rice case back in 2014 so the  commissioner who spoke somewhat  extensively in the rice case he had that  press conference and maybe didn't go all  that well and i think they've realized  you know what  there's a chance that whatever he says  may make it worse so let's just have him  say nothing and there is a sensitivity  the way i understand it there was a  willingness on sunday night to come out  and acknowledge the error and privately  they've admitted it privately the the  nfl has communicated to the saints that  yes they screwed it up  it's one thing to admit it privately  it's another thing to admit it publicly  because then you admit it publicly and  what happens the rams are delegitimized  as super bowl participants so there was  a thought at one point they were going  to mention in this statement well we got  the non-call  wrong as it relates to past interference  but we also missed a face mask that was  committed against jared goff in the  prior drive so it kind of evens out they  decided not to do that they've just  decided to do nothing now they they  leaked to some reporters the idea that  that there would be potential solutions  via expansion of replay review but in  the aftermath of that you had the people  on the bunch of people on the record  connected to the league including  competition committee chairman rich  mckay saying  that won't work  so  where are we five days later we're  nowhere we're back to where we started  and i think the league at this point  just hopes it all goes away that they  get a hard reset going into super bowl  week but the problem is  one of the super bowl contenders is the  team that benefited from that bad  non-call so  whenever the commissioner has his press  conference it used to be on friday when  maximum media was in the super bowl city  they have now moved it back to wednesday  they've done that for several years now  and and the stated reason is they don't  want the commissioners press conference  to overshadow the the run up to the  super bowl the reality is you've got far  fewer people in the media  who are there  earlier in the week so wednesday at 1 pm  eastern is when the commissioner will be  speaking and what someone needs to do  and this is not an easy thing to do  i made the analogy earlier in the  program to the one ring and i know  that's an extremely nerdy reference and  i apologize in advance but let me tell  you when you get that one ring in your  hand and you're standing in mount doom  over the fires and you got to throw it  in there and destroy it right good luck  doing it because when you get that  microphone in your hand at the  commissioner's press conference  even if you have it scripted out a very  concise and narrow question that puts  the commissioner on the spot puts him in  a position where all he can say is yes  or no  you're gonna end up instead asking a  question with a 45 second preamble that  has five parts to it because you got  that microphone in your hand and the  last thing you're doing is throwing that  thing into the fires of mount doom and  i've been there i can relate and every  year it happens so  someone in that if if they if they  happen to give the microphone to someone  that that may ask the question because  let me tell you that microphone is going  to get selectively passed around to  people who will never ask the question  if it ends up in the hands of someone  who may ask the question we'll see if if  that person manages to pull the sword  out of the stone and ask the direct  question and get a direct answer a  non-evasive answer a real answer an  honest answer about whether or not there  was an error and what the league will do  moving forward to improve the situation  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports
 

